res you  have global denial of service attacks  you have BGP hijacks you have  authentication system failures you have  configuration changes that went awry  right none of these things are actually  related in hardware none of them would  cause the data center to look like a  tornado had gone through it the data  store would look identical  yet the impact on on your service and  impact on that particular zone region is  the same right everything stops working  so you know while we're on this topic we  can actually talk a little bit about you  know current events right we can learn  from what's happened with the curve 8:19  pandemic with kovat we saw you know  another unexpected event that really  caused no damage to the physical  infrastructure but caused huge changes  in the usage pattern from users and for  here at Google we saw for example huge  increases in video conferencing use with  our meat product and while we were able  to scale up and meet that demand through  moving resources around we also saw  other instances in industry where demand  clearly had surged and the providers or  the services struggled to meet the  increased demand and that was if you  will a failure of scalability or a  failure of flexibility still a logical  failure rather than physical failure but  one more example of why that are the the  things that you should worry about are  not the things that look dramatic or are  up here in your mind's eye so much as  the logical problems that actually are a  bit more mundane but really dominate the  downtime that we measure the data so  coming back to this discussion of zones  and regions the bottom line is not that  physical failure to never happen if they  do but rather that they're not actually  what the data tells us we should focus  on because they are de minimis in terms  of their impact on your application and  logical failures are really the thing  that we must pay attention to so for  those services that actually require  physical separation or physical  isolation for for example regulatory  reasons we actually recommend that you  look at using multiple regions rather  than multiple zones because both regions  tend to be physically more distinct than  zones where it's owned by average with  public cloud providers  about 10 kilometers apart from each  other regions tend to be hundreds of  kilometers apart from each other and so  you get more physical redundancy for  much of the same logical work that's  required to make your application run on  multiple locations the other reason that  we recommend multiple regions however is  that on all of the providers the logical  isolation that you get between different  regions is significantly higher than the  logical isolation that you get between  zones and the same region and so in  practice what we see is that an  application that runs in multiple  regions will have many fewer logical  failures that it's exposed to than an  application that simply runs in multiple  zones inside the same region ok so at  this point you've got your user journeys  described you've got your s ellos and  you're working on this question of  should I use multiple zones or show you  multiple regions or is a single zone  sufficient for my needs you also at this  point want to consider how easy the  provider makes it to deploy instances of  your application across multiple  locations whether it's zones or regions  you know generally all of the public  cloud providers offer regional versions  of their services that automatically  distribute the data and processing user  traffic across the multiple zones in  region and you can use their offering or  you can roll your own but generally what  we would recommend is that you use the  versions of those tools provided by your  provider for two reasons right one is  they have many more flight models on  them than whatever you're going to build  yourself and two is you benefit from all  of the other customers who are also  using those tools 