 to select a multi  regional offering and that do data  replication and data processing across  multiple regions and then we have a very  large very high bandwidth backbone  network that allows you to transfer data  between regions and do load balancing to  distribute your traffic across multiple  regions so putting this all together you  know what do you want to look for in a  cloud provider and how do you want to  think about your architecture for  account providers right I would say  first and foremost you should ignore the  marketing and look at the data the  performance data from the different  cloud providers is different  and each of them has some pros and some  cons and you want to look at how those  pros and cons would affect the  application that you're trying on and  the availability targets that you've  chosen for yourself  and pick one that's the best match for  your particular needs I would say  secondly you want to look at the  strengths of each provider in terms of  their infrastructures security right  infrastructure attacks themselves are a  reliability problem if there's a DDoS  and no one really cares whether it's  your application or your providers  infrastructure that failed to protect  you right all the users will know is  their application stopped working and  they're angry with you and so it's  important to look at the track record of  dealing with large-scale attacks and  large scale security challenges and make  sure that the infrastructure providers  that you are using meet your needs in  those regards you should look at the  size maturity and scalability of the  providers infrastructure again picking  on denial of service attacks right those  attacks tend to be very large there are  public reports of attacks in the multi  terabit range and so size does matter  and scale doesn't matter  and picking providers that have both the  resources in the track record to be able  to absorb very large attacks when they  happen will give you a degree 