 we minimize  failures we also think about how do we  make our service resilient do the sorts  of failures that we're likely to see  when they do happen and we spend as much  time on systems here at Google which  respond to failures and mask them from  our users as we do in preventing those  failures in the first place in this  principle that failures are normal but  we want a good user experience despite a  small but continuous level of failures  and systems pretty much affects all the  phases of system design development and  deployment so I'll talk about two the  most important areas there architecture  and design and then deployment and  operation so for architecture and design  we recommend taking a sceptical position  right and choosing a architecture which  breaks your stack into components those  components can be decoupled from each  other they can be moved around  they can be scaled up and down and this  is something that cloud makes it very  easy to do my cloud already offers  scalable and flexible capacity it offers  multiple independent zones and regions  if you have a modern application design  using micro services right we have  products like those that make it easy to  make components mobile to scale them and  also makes it easy to detect problems  with individual components and onion on  them if you have a more traditional  application that ones and virtual  machines Google compute engine has  managed instance groups which gives you  a similar level of flexibility and  finally the idea of getting resilience  through failure detection and daily  minimization is a key concept right if  you have load balancing that can shift  traffic between different pools of  instances then you can send small  amounts of user traffic for example to a  new version of the application and  verify that those users are getting the  result you expect before you increase  the fraction of your application that  uses that new version and that way you  can seamlessly roll users over to a new  version of the application without ever  to do too many of them at the risk of a  new release at once and if it isn't  working the way you expect you can just  send over your traffic back to the  existing instances that you've got the  version of your application that no  works and then you can debug what went  wrong at your leisure without time  pressure during development cloud also  offers a number of powerful tools to  support best practices like continuous  build and testing for example it's very  easy to spin up a whole new test  instance of your production application  that's decoupled from your actual  production environment and there you can  test new versions of components make  sure everything works together doing  integration tests and spinning back down  and that way by the time you actually  deploy to production you have a great  deal of confidence that the application  is going to work the way you want and  you've taken advantage of the same  concepts of  component groups and scaling that you'll  use in your production environment so  when we talk about deployment and  operation I think of the different areas  of reliability this one often gets less  attention than it ought and I'm not just  saying that because I've spent the last  two decades of my life working on  deployment problems but for two reasons  really primarily that the impact of a  change on an application is somewhat  underappreciated most failures that we  see occurred during attempts to change  in application it's a single largest  positive outage and yet it's not the  place sometimes where we spend the most  time making sure that our practices are  safe and appropriate to the reliability  Karadzic's that we've got and secondly  because change management is actually  hard and historically it's been  difficult to be sophisticated about  change management it can be treated as  sort of a monolithic we have the old  version we have the new version you have  a failover period and that approach is  very very high-stakes you have to get  everything right with the new version  where you have a catastrophe and so  really when we think about deployment  and operation it is a place to take  advantage of a lot of the capabilities  that cloud gives you to drastically  reduce the risk of deployment and the  risk of new versions and then in turn  means that you can deploy much more  frequently and have higher development  velocity and eventually have any more  features for your users which is  obviously a highly desirable trait so a  few tips and this is a deep area so I'm  just going to scratch the surface but  shameless plug for the SRA book if you  want to read a lot more about this in  general the best way to manage a change  and thus minimize the risk of a change  was edit outage is first of all to  practice progressive little loud right  and progressive rollout means that you  apply updates to instances of your  application either in different zones  and regions independently or to small  fractions of your instances first and  then greater  actions as you gain confidence that  everything is working the way you expect  and cloud infrastructure supports you  here both with automated development  tools but also with automated deployment  tools supervision of rollouts is very  very important and supervision in  particular means knowing what you expect  to see and only continuing a little  rollout if the results are exactly what  you expect right no more nor less and  they're different a lot of times what we  see with rollouts that have gone bad is  they weren't overtly failing but they  weren't behaving the way that we  expected and the criteria for continuing  had de facto then well unless it's  breaking keep rolling out right and and  that masks things like oops  you know we're consuming six percent  more memory than we had expected which  is fine at the time you're doing the  rollout during a local trough of traffic  but the next morning in peak traffic  rolls around it turns out that's a  problem right and so this idea of  knowing what to expect and only  proceeding with a rollout when it's  working the way to expect gives you a  great deal of safety for really  extremely low cost and then in addition  to roll out obviously if things aren't  working well your roll backs need to be  fast safe and easy right some of the  outage post-mortems that we've done one  of the reasons that the object occurred  was that the team that was in the middle  of the rollout was nervous about rolling  back when the system wasn't behaving the  way they expected and that has a  predictable result right if your roll  backs are fast and safe and your  application was working before you  started changing it right then hitting  the big rollback button the first moment  that something isn't doing the way you  expect becomes habit for everyone and  that actually it will save you from a  ton of affordable outages and then  finally designing your applications so  that it fails in a way that gives you a  reasonable result we call it fails  static but things like you know you get  a configuration change and you send that  configuration change to the first  instance of your app and that instance  of the app stops responding we're  for whatever reason maybe it's crashing  loop in the crash loop maybe it's just  not able to parse the configuration you  know whatever the application at that  point can say well I'm not sure what's  going on but I know that was working  fine before this change started so I'm  just gonna stop applying changes to the  app fail static and fire an alarm that  asks the human to come to look for me  and this is this idea of failed static  can save you from a great number of  unexpected behaviors during a rollout or  during a change with essentially no cost  again to your user because the  application just keeping used to work  the way it used to work and cloud can  really help you in a number of these  practices because it offers scalable  infrastructure so that you can spin up  and spin down instances you know during  the rollout you can have extra capacity  to run both new and old side-by-side for  example it allows you to test it allows  you to monitor the behavior of your  application and it gives you insights  into the behavior individual components  so that you can look at you know is it  working the way it used to work not just  at the SLO level and for your user  journeys but actually at the component  level and this gives you a great deal of  safety so in summary that was a  whirlwind tour of reliability in the  cloud it's like I said my my life's work  at this point  and it's a very very big topic but we  have at least covered a number of the  most important and most salient points  for building and running reliable  applications in this lab in the cloud  and my goal today really was to give you  a framework about thinking about these  three major steps in your cloud  reliability journey and then three to  those steps to give you some specific  ideas to get you started down the  process of more detailed planning  developing for reliability in the cloud  can require some new thinking and a  change in perspective but it also gives  you a great deal of flexibility and a  great deal of power and I can assure you  from experience it is a step function  right once you've looked at building  reliable applications this way you won't  look at them in the  way very often anymore and I hope this  has been helpful for you both in  thinking through that process thinking  through assessing your application and  your measurable availability goals  thinking through how to select a public  cloud provider or providers and  leveraging the systems and tools and  structures that they have available to  make your application more reliable and  finally to help you think about  architecture design deployment and  operation of your application to  anticipate and work through of life  